  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nOur weather pattern has changed little over the last few days, \nthat is why we have seen similar weather with thunderstorms each \nnight. The players that have been giving us this weather is an \nupper level ridge centered over the southeast U.S. and an upper \nlevel trough centered over California. On Friday, the upper level \ntrough will slowly begin to push toward the east across the Great \nBasin. This will help push our plume of moisture eastward. The \nthreat for thunderstorms will continue for most of the area on \nFriday, but the threat for heavier rain will shift east of the Rio\nGrande. As the upper level trough pushes into the upper Midwest \non Saturday, drier air will flow in behind it and that will lower \nour rain chances on Saturday and by Sunday we will have \nprecipitable water values (PW's) well below an inch, so we may be \nrain free on Sunday. By Monday another upper level trough builds \ninto southern California and that begins to draw moisture back \ninto our region. But it will be a slow tap of moisture, so Monday \nis also looking pretty dry. But by Tuesday, we will again between \nan upper level ridge to our east and an upper level trough to our \nwest and the circulations around the two will pull up abundant \nmoisture up out of Mexico. Tuesday through Thursday of next week \nlooks very rainy. It is still unclear how much instability we will\nhave, but if we have some thunderstorms we will see a return to \nthe threat of flash flooding, but if we just have a lot of rain \nshowers, it will be cool and damp, but the chances for flooding \nwill be lower.\n\nTaking a quick look at temperatures. For Friday through Monday, \nwe will see temperatures running near or a little above average. \nBut then as the moisture returns, we will see our high \ntemperatures drop below average for the middle of next week. High \ntemperatures could run 3 to 6 degrees below average by next \nWednesday.",
